🧠 Models
This directory contains the computational pipelines and deep learning frameworks used for the classification of head and neck lymphoid lesions using histopathological image analysis,
nuclear morphometric features, and clinicopathologic data.

The repository includes:
- UNI foundation model pipeline
- CellViT++ nuclear segmentation framework
- Multimodal deep learning models
- SHAP explainability analysis
- Metrics and evaluation utilities
The framework was developed for multicentre histopathological datasets and follows TRIPOD-AI and STARD-AI recommendations for AI-based diagnostic studies. 

🧩 Directory Structure
models/
├── uni_pipeline.py
├── cellvitpp_pipeline.py
├── shap_analysis.py
├── metrics.py
├── data_split.py
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md

🔬 UNI Foundation Model Pipeline

The UNI pipeline implements a transformer-based foundation model framework for histopathological image representation learning. Feature extraction is performed using the UNI Vision 
Transformer (vit_large_patch16_224) operating as a frozen encoder without weight updates.

├── Main characteristics
─ End-to-end WSI classification
─ Recursive slide discovery
─ Patch extraction from WSIs
─ Attention-based MIL aggregation
─ Patch-level and patient-level inference
─ Heatmap generation
─ Bootstrap confidence intervals
─ Multi-class classification

├── Input
─ Whole-slide images (.svs)
─ Histopathological patches (224 × 224 RGB)

├── Output
─ Patch embeddings
─ MIL attention maps
─ ROC curves
─ Confusion matrices
─ Classification reports
─ Heatmaps
─ Cached feature tensors

├── Architecture
─ Patch-level classifier
Linear(d → 256)
ReLU
Dropout(0.3)
Linear(256 → 4)

─ MIL attention mechanism
Linear(d → 128)
Tanh
Linear(128 → 1)
Softmax

- Slide-level classifier
Linear(d → 128)
ReLU
Dropout(0.2)
Linear(128 → 4)
The UNI configuration and extracted features are fully described in Supplementary Table 7.

🔬 CellViT++ Nuclear Segmentation Pipeline

The CellViT++ framework was used for instance-level nuclear segmentation and downstream morphometric feature extraction. The original architecture was used without modifications to preserve 
reproducibility and comparability.

├── Main characteristics
- Vision Transformer–based segmentation
- Nuclear instance detection
- Morphometric feature extraction
- Binary mask generation
- Extracted morphometric features
- Nuclear area
- Nuclear perimeter
- Circularity
- Eccentricity
Morphometric feature extraction and segmentation details are described in Supplementary Table 6.

├── Segmentation performance
The segmentation framework demonstrated robust performance across heterogeneous tissue samples:
─ Dice coefficient: 0.91 (range: 0.86–0.95)
─ IoU: 0.84 (range: 0.78–0.90)

🧬 Multimodal Deep Learning Framework
The multimodal framework integrates:
─ Histopathological image embeddings
─ Nuclear morphometric descriptors
─ Clinicopathologic variables

├── The fusion strategy combines AlexNet-derived image representations with MLP embeddings generated from morphometric and clinical variables.
─ Architecture
─ CNN backbone
─ Clinical variables
─ Age
─ Sex
─ Anatomical location
─ Morphometric variables
─ Nuclear area
─ Perimeter
─ Circularity
─ Eccentricity

🧠 Explainability Analysis

├── Model interpretability was evaluated using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations).

The analysis demonstrated that:
─ Nuclear morphometric features dominate the model decision space
─ Nuclear area, perimeter, and eccentricity presented the highest SHAP values
─ Clinical variables showed comparatively lower contribution
─ Morphometric descriptors aligned with WHO diagnostic criteria
These findings support the biological plausibility and interpretability of the multimodal framework.

⚙️ Training Configuration
─ Computational environment
Ubuntu 20.04.1 LTS
Python 3.12.11
PyTorch 2.8.0
CUDA 12.8
Intel Xeon W-2295 CPU
125 GB RAM
3 × NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPUs (24 GB each)

─ Training settings
Optimizer: AdamW
Learning rate: 1 × 10⁻⁴
Weight decay: 1 × 10⁻⁴
Loss function: Weighted Cross-Entropy
Mixed precision training
Multi-GPU support (torch.nn.DataParallel)

📊 Evaluation Metrics
─ The framework supports:
Accuracy
Weighted precision
Weighted recall
Weighted F1-score
Balanced accuracy
Cohen’s kappa
Sensitivity
Specificity
ROC AUC
Confusion matrices
Calibration metrics
Bootstrap confidence intervals

🌎 External Validation
─ The dataset was partitioned into:
Training cohort
Validation cohort
Internal test cohort
External validation cohort A
External validation cohort B
Data splitting was performed in a center-aware manner to minimise data leakage and ensure robust multicentre validation.

🧪 AI-Guided Diagnostic Workflow
The probabilistic outputs generated by the multimodal framework were integrated into a structured diagnostic support workflow guiding immunohistochemical and molecular testing.

─ The workflow supports:
First-line IHC panel recommendation
Reflex panel escalation
Molecular testing recommendation
Confidence-aware decision support
The framework acts as a diagnostic support layer intended to augment — not replace — specialist pathologist interpretation.
